<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Kabir Das</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabir was an Indian mystic poet and saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> whose verses influenced the Bhakti movement; some historians place his life in 1398–1448, others in 1440–1518. A few accounts say he was raised by a Muslim weaver and his wife, and he is believed to have been a disciple of Ramananda. His songs and couplets, composed orally and gathered in collections such as the Bijak, attacked the hypocrisy and misguided rituals he saw in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> alike. Verses attributed to him form the largest non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> contribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabir.jpg" alt="Kabir" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabir004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The years of Kabir's birth and death are uncertain, and some historians favour 1398–1448 while others favour 1440–1518. Followers hold that he came from Satloka and appeared on a lotus flower. A few accounts say that as an infant he was found at Lahartara Lake by a Muslim weaver named Niru and his wife Nima, who raised him. He is believed to have become a disciple of the Bhakti poet-saint Ramananda in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, and the Persian text Dabestan-e Mazaheb, the earliest known text with biographical information, calls him a Bairagi, a Vaishnava yogi, and a disciple of Ramanand. His family is believed to have lived at Kabir Chaura in Varanasi.</p><p>Kabir's couplets suggest that he was persecuted for his views while alive, and he was said to have been threatened by both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims. Legends describe him as an underdog who triumphs in trials before a sultan or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>, but many scholars doubt them, pointing to the lack of corroborating evidence. When he died, Hindus and Muslims both claimed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kabir composed his poems orally in Sadhukkadi, a mixed language drawing on Khadi boli, Braj, Bhojpuri, Marwari and Awadhi, in simple words. He and his followers called them bāṇīs, utterances, and they include songs and couplets such as the dohe and the sākhī, a word meaning witness. Collections attributed to him include the Kabir Bijak, the Sakhi Granth and the Kabir Granthawali, and his verses form the largest non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> contribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a>. Except for the Adi Granth, the versions differ considerably, and the Bijak was first written down in the seventeenth century, so scholars debate which poems are authentic.</p><p>Kabir attacked what he regarded as the follies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> alike and questioned the need for any holy book. Linda Hess holds that he is falsely presented as a synthesizer of the two religions, since he declared his independence from both. He taught that God is within every person and urged people to drop the ego. The Kabir panth, a Sant Mat sect that recognises him as its founder, arose centuries after his death, over the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, and two temples to him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, one maintained by Hindus and one by Muslims. Rabindranath Tagore's Songs of Kabir was first published in 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hinduism remained the religion of most of the population, and the long encounter produced conflict and destruction of temples but also exchange: the bhakti poets and the Sufis shared ideas, and figures such as <b>Kabir</b> drew on both traditions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It was first compiled by Guru Arjan in 1604 as the Adi Granth, the “first book”, from the hymns of the first five Gurus and of saints of other traditions, among them the weaver-poet <b>Kabir</b>, the Sufi Shaikh Farid, the leatherworker Ravidas and the tailor Namdev.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Others, the sants of northern India such as <b>Kabir</b> and Ravidas, worshipped God without form or attributes, nirguna, whom no image or scripture could contain, in contrast to the saguna devotion to a god with form.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the sultans and the Mughals the bhakti poets and the Sufis shared ideas, and <b>Kabir</b> drew on both traditions.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Maghar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1449" aria-label="Varanasi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6749 1232 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kabir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7199 1502h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7199 1502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1449">What was here in 1449? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O servant, where dost thou seek Me? Lo! I am beside thee. I am neither in temple nor in mosque: I am neither in Kaaba nor in Kailash…&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir, I &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; the voice is God's, speaking in the poem &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is but folly to ask what the caste of a saint may be; The barber has sought God, the washerwoman, and the carpenter— Even Raidas was a seeker after God.&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir, II &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is nothing but water at the holy bathing places; and I know that they are useless, for I have bathed in them. The images are all lifeless, they cannot speak; I know, for I have cried aloud to them.&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir, XLII &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If God be within the mosque, then to whom does this world belong?&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I laugh when I hear that the fish in the water is thirsty…&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The moon shines in my body, but my blind eyes cannot see it…&#8221;</p><footer>Songs of Kabir &#183; tr. Rabindranath Tagore, 1915 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6519/pg6519-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
